Observability: SDD

Purpose:
  Define observability requirements for both the system being built
  and the AI execution system itself. Observability covers logs,
  metrics, traces, events, audit, decision provenance, cost tracking,
  and quality analysis for AI-driven development.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  System observability (logs, metrics, traces, monitoring)
  AI execution observability (decision trace, context trace, policy trace)
  Cost tracking and token efficiency
  Tool and agent performance metrics
  Skill performance metrics
  Event architecture (event bus, envelope, versioning)
  Audit and compliance
  Data classification and secret redaction
  Budget policy and alerting
  Root cause analysis and failure taxonomy

Principles:
  Observability MUST be designed into the system, not added later.
  Logs, metrics, and traces are complementary, not interchangeable.
  Alert on symptoms, not causes.
  Every service MUST expose health checks.
  AI decision provenance MUST be reproducible.
  Sensitive data MUST be redacted in all observability outputs.
  Observability data MUST be classified (PUBLIC, INTERNAL, CONFIDENTIAL, SECRET).

SystemObservability:
  Logs:
    levels:
      ERROR: system errors requiring immediate attention
      WARN: potential problems, degraded functionality
      INFO: significant state changes, business events
      DEBUG: detailed diagnostic information

    requirements:
      structured: JSON format with timestamp, level, service, trace_id
      context: include request_id, user_id (anonymized), operation
      correlation: trace_id MUST link logs to traces
      sensitive_data: MUST NOT log PII, secrets, tokens
      volume: log volume MUST be bounded and monitored

    retention:
      hot: 7 days (searchable)
      warm: 30 days (slower search)
      cold: 1 year (compliance archive)

  Metrics:
    types:
      counter: cumulative value (requests_total, errors_total)
      gauge: point-in-time value (queue_depth, active_connections)
      histogram: distribution (request_duration_seconds)
      summary: streaming quantiles

    golden_signals:
      latency: request duration (p50, p95, p99)
      traffic: requests per second
      errors: error rate (4xx, 5xx)
      saturation: resource utilization (CPU, memory, connections)

    retention:
      high resolution (1s): 1 hour
      medium resolution (1m): 7 days
      low resolution (5m): 90 days

  Traces:
    requirements:
      every request MUST generate trace_id
      trace_id MUST propagate across service boundaries
      spans MUST include service name, operation, start/end time
      spans MUST link to parent span_id
      sampling rate MUST be configurable

    sampling:
      production: 1-10% of requests
      error traces: 100% of failed requests

  Monitoring:
    health_checks:
      liveness: process alive, basic functionality
      readiness: dependencies available, cache warm
      startup: migrations complete, cache populated

    dashboards:
      service_dashboard: golden signals, SLO compliance, recent deploys
      infrastructure_dashboard: CPU, memory, disk, network
      business_dashboard: key business metrics, user activity

  Alerting:
    principles:
      alert_on_symptoms: SLO at risk, user experience degraded
      actionable: every alert has runbook and owner
      noise_reduction: group, deduplicate, suppress during deploys

    severity:
      page: SLO at risk, service down, data loss, security incident
      warn: degraded performance, approaching SLO
      info: deploy completed, capacity planning

AIObservability:
  DecisionTrace:
    definition:
      record of why AI made a specific decision

    structure:
      decision:
        type: DATABASE_SELECTION
        candidates: [PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB]
        selected: PostgreSQL
        constraints: ACID required, relational model required
        source: ADR-012
        factors: [relational_data, transaction_support, existing_stack]

    rule: system records decision summary, not full chain-of-thought
    rule: decision trace enables reproducibility

  ContextTrace:
    definition:
      record of what context was used for a task

    structure:
      context:
        task: TASK-100
        files: [user.go, repository.go]
        docs: [auth.md]
        decisions: [ADR-12]
        policies: [SEC-01]

    rule: context trace is recorded with execution

  PolicyTrace:
    definition:
      record of which policies were evaluated and their decisions

    structure:
      policy_snapshot: POLICY-SNAPSHOT-20
      decisions:
        - policy: DEV-WRITE-01
          result: ALLOW
          reason: resource_in_write_scope

  ContextSnapshot:
    definition:
      immutable snapshot of context at execution time

    structure:
      snapshot_id: CTX-100
      execution: EXEC-500
      sdd_version: 15
      source_revision: abc123
      items: [API-10, DOMAIN-AUTH, ARCH-5, auth/login.go]

  PolicySnapshot:
    definition:
      immutable snapshot of active policies at execution time

    structure:
      snapshot_id: POLICY-SNAPSHOT-20
      execution: EXEC-500
      policies: [DEV-WRITE-01, SEC-AUTH-01, ...]

  AIExecutionFingerprint:
    definition:
      hash of all execution dependencies for reproducibility

    structure:
      fingerprint:
        agent_version: 3.2.1
        skill_version: 2.1
        prompt_version: 7
        model_version: 1.5
        context_snapshot: CTX-100
        policy_snapshot: POLICY-SNAPSHOT-20

CostTracking:
  metrics:
    input_tokens
    output_tokens
    total_tokens
    estimated_cost

  example:
    EXEC-500:
      input = 120k
      output = 18k
      cost = $0.82

  cost_per_task:
    TASK-AUTH: average_cost = $0.31
    TASK-PAYMENT: average_cost = $2.80

  cost_per_agent:
    AGENT-CODER: $500/month
    AGENT-QA: $180/month
    AGENT-ARCHITECT: $220/month

TokenEfficiency:
  metric:
    task_success / token_usage

  rule: high token usage for simple tasks indicates skill/context problems

ToolPerformance:
  metrics:
    tool_calls_total
    tool_failures
    average_latency
    timeout_rate
    retry_rate

  example:
    go_test: failure_rate = 12%

AgentPerformance:
  metrics:
    success_rate
    failure_rate
    average_steps
    average_tokens
    average_cost
    average_duration
    retry_rate

SkillPerformance:
  metrics:
    success_rate
    average_duration
    failure_rate

  example:
    SKILL-REPOSITORY-PATTERN: success_rate = 96%
    SKILL-LEGACY-MIGRATION: success_rate = 61%

  rule: skill performance drives improvement decisions

ObservabilityFeedbackLoop:
  flow:
    Execution -> Observability -> Metrics -> Analysis -> Skill Improvement -> New Version -> Execution

  rule: system can self-improve based on observability
  rule: auto-changes require governance

DataClassification:
  levels:
    PUBLIC: no restrictions
    INTERNAL: internal use only
    CONFIDENTIAL: restricted access
    SECRET: highly sensitive

  rule: all observability data MUST be classified
  rule: SECRET data is never exposed in logs or traces

SecretRedaction:
  rule: secrets MUST be redacted in all outputs
  example:
    Authorization: Bearer eyJ... -> Authorization: [REDACTED]
    DATABASE_URL=postgres://... -> DATABASE_URL=[REDACTED]

  rule: observability system must not create security problems

BudgetPolicy:
  structure:
    budget:
      max_tokens: 200000
      max_cost: $2
      max_duration: 30m
      max_tool_calls: 100

  rule: budget exhaustion triggers PAUSE or DENY
  rule: budget policy is enforced by policy engine

EventArchitecture:
  EventEnvelope:
    structure:
      event:
        id: string
        type: string
        version: string
        timestamp: ISO 8601
        correlation_id: string
        causation_id: string
        actor: string
        source: string
        payload: object

  EventVersioning:
    rule: event schemas are versioned
    example:
      ToolCalled.v1
      ToolCalled.v2

    rule: consumers MUST handle old event versions

  EventProducer:
    examples:
      Execution Engine: ExecutionStarted
      Tool Runner: ToolCompleted

  EventConsumer:
    examples:
      Observability Service: metrics, traces
      Audit Service: audit log
      Notification Service: alerts

  EventBus:
    purpose: decouple producers from consumers
    properties:
      ordering: per execution_id
      delivery: at-least-once
      retry: with backoff
      dead-letter: DLQ for failed events
      idempotency: event_id based deduplication
      partitioning: by execution_id
      retention: configurable

  DuplicateEvents:
    rule: consumers MUST be idempotent
    rule: deduplication by event_id

  DeadLetterQueue:
    rule: failed events go to DLQ
    rule: DLQ is monitored and reprocessed

  Ordering:
    rule: ordering is required per execution_id
    rule: global ordering is NOT required (too expensive)

  EventCategories:
    DomainEvents:
      UserCreated, PaymentCompleted, OrderCancelled

    SystemEvents:
      ExecutionStarted, ToolCalled, ToolFailed, AgentAssigned, PolicyDenied

EndToEndTrace:
  structure:
    REQ-100
      ↓
    TASK-200
      ↓
    EXEC-300
      ↓
    ├── AGENT-10
      │     ├── SKILL-20
      │     ├── CTX-30
      │     └── PROMPT-40
      ├── POLICY-50
      ├── ACTION-60
      │     └── TOOL-70
      ├── VALIDATION-80
      └── ARTIFACT-90

  rule: every node has ID, Version, Timestamp, Actor, Parent, Correlation

Provenance:
  definition:
    record of how a result was produced

  example:
    Artifact-90:
      created_by: EXEC-300
      derived_from: TASK-200
      implements: REQ-100

RootCauseAnalysis:
  flow:
    Task failed
      -> trace back through execution
      -> identify root cause
      -> example: root_cause = TOOL_TIMEOUT

FailureTaxonomy:
  categories:
    LLM_FAILURE
    TOOL_FAILURE
    NETWORK_FAILURE
    POLICY_DENIED
    VALIDATION_FAILURE
    TIMEOUT
    CONTEXT_FAILURE
    DEPENDENCY_FAILURE
    USER_INPUT_REQUIRED
    SYSTEM_FAILURE

  rule: failures are categorized, not just logged as errors

Alerting:
  ai_specific_alerts:
    - agent_retry_rate > 5
    - token_usage > expected_budget
    - execution_failure_rate > 20%
    - production_policy_denials > threshold

  rule: alerts are actionable and have owners

Navigation:
  Architecture: @../architecture/INDEX.sdd
  Runtime: @../runtime/INDEX.sdd
  Events: @../runtime/events.sdd
  Execution: @../runtime/execution.sdd
  Workflow: @../workflow/INDEX.sdd
  Protocol: @../protocol/INDEX.sdd

Rules:
  [OBS-01] Observability MUST be designed into the system, not added later.
  [OBS-02] AI decision provenance MUST be reproducible.
  [OBS-03] Sensitive data MUST be redacted in all observability outputs.
  [OBS-04] Observability data MUST be classified (PUBLIC, INTERNAL, CONFIDENTIAL, SECRET).
  [OBS-05] Events are immutable and append-only.
  [OBS-06] Event ordering is guaranteed per execution_id.
  [OBS-07] Event consumers MUST be idempotent.
  [OBS-08] Decision trace records summary and factors, not full chain-of-thought.
  [OBS-09] Cost and token metrics are required for AI operations.
  [OBS-10] Skill performance metrics drive improvement decisions.
  [OBS-11] Failure taxonomy enables better alerting and analysis.
  [OBS-12] Budget policy is enforced during execution.
  [OBS-13] End-to-end trace links Requirement to Artifact.
  [OBS-14] Root cause analysis traces failures through execution chain.
  [OBS-15] Agent MUST NOT add signatures or sign-offs to observability outputs.
  [OBS-16] Agent MUST NOT use emojis, icons, or decorative symbols in observability output headers or titles — reserved for human use only.

State: +

  